---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ReactionGame from '../components/ReactionGame.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Reaction Time Test: Five-Round Reflex Test" description="Take a five-round online reaction time test. Each valid click is measured in milliseconds, then summarized with best, median, and average scores.">
  <section class="article-hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Interactive test</p><h1>Reaction Time Test</h1><p class="lede">Run five valid reaction rounds and review the session with a best, median, and average result. Early clicks do not count.</p></div></section>
  <div class="game-wrap"><ReactionGame /></div>
  <article class="article-page article-copy">
    <h2>A five-round test, not a one-click claim</h2>
    <p>This page uses the same original reaction game as the homepage, but the purpose is narrower: complete five clean responses, then inspect the session. One response alone can be unusual. The median helps describe the center of the five values, the average includes every score, and the best highlights the fastest valid response.</p>
    <p>Start each round only when you are ready to pay attention. The panel waits for a random interval before it turns green. Clicking while it is still waiting creates a false start; it does not become a fast score and does not enter the results. Click once after green, then use the panel again to prepare the next response.</p>
    <h2>Read the result in context</h2>
    <p>The number shown is the time observed by your browser between its visual signal and its input event. It is useful for repeating the same exercise on the same setup. It does not separate a personal response from display timing, input timing, the operating system, or browser scheduling. Use it as a personal game result, not as a standardized or clinical measurement.</p>
    <p>For a more consistent comparison, use the same browser, device, display, and input method. A touchscreen run and a desktop-mouse run are different setups. The score guide explains why the page does not attach unsupported labels such as “excellent” or publish a universal score table.</p>
    <p class="article-callout">The site does not send scores to a server or maintain a public ranking. A copied result remains a choice made from your own browser.</p>
  </article>
  <SourceList />
</BaseLayout>
